[{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q1","number":1,"question":"<p>A ball is thrown vertically upwards with a speed of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"19.6\\text{ms}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The height of the ball, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\" data-display=\"false\"></span> metres, may be modelled by the equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=19.6t-4.9t^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the time given in seconds.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the height of the ball after 2 seconds.</p></li><li><p>Find the times at which the ball is at a height of 14.7 metres above the ground.</p></li><li><p>Explain what your two solutions in the previous part represent.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=19.6\\times2-4.9\\times4=19.6\" data-display=\"false\"></span> m</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"14.7=19.6t-4.9t^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4.9t^2-19.6t+14.7=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=\\frac{19.6\\pm\\sqrt{384.16-288.12}}{9.8}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=1\\text{s},t=3\\text{s}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is when it reaches a height 14.7m on the way up and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=3\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is when it drops back to the height of 14.7m on the way back down.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q2","number":2,"question":"<p>A ball is hit into the air, the graph below shows its vertical height above the ground before it hits the ground.</p><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.11/questions/assets/diagrams/q2.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><p>A model for the height of the ball, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\" data-display=\"false\"></span> metres at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds is given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h(t)=10t-2t^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Find</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>the time when the ball hits the ground.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k(t-p)^2+q\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constants to be found.</p></li><li><p>the maximum height the ball reaches and the time at which it reaches the maximum height.</p></li></ol><p>An improved model starts the ball at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"4\"><li><p>State the new model in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h(t)=at^2+bt+c\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>State the coordinate of the maximum height of the curve of this new model.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0=2t(5-t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=5\\text{s}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-2t^2+10t=-2(t^2-5t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=-2[(t-\\frac52)^2-(-\\frac52)^2]=-2(t-\\frac52)^2+\\frac{25}2\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>Max at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(\\frac52,\\frac{25}2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so max height is 12.5m after 2.5s has passed.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-2t^2+10t+1\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(\\frac52,\\frac{27}2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q3","number":3,"question":"<p>Two identical fence posts are 2.5 metres apart and a rope is hung between them. The curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=\\frac1{100}x^2-\\frac1{40}x+\\frac12\" data-display=\"false\"></span> may be used to model the rope, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the vertical distance in metres above the ground and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the horizontal distance in metres from the left post. The curve is shown in the diagram below:</p><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.11/questions/assets/diagrams/q3.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>State the height of the posts.</p></li><li><p>Rearrange the model into the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a(x-b)^2+c\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"c\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constants to be found.</p></li><li><p>State the horizontal and vertical position of the minimum height of the rope.</p></li><li><p>State why the model is not effective for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x&gt;2.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The posts are 0.5m high</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac1{100}x^2-\\frac1{40}x+\\frac12=\\frac1{100}[x^2-\\frac52x+50]\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=\\frac1{100}[(x-\\frac54)^2-(-\\frac54)^2+50]\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=\\frac1{100}[(x-\\frac54)^2+\\frac{775}{16}]=\\frac1{100}(x-\\frac54)^2+\\frac{31}{64}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>Horizontal position of the minimum height of the rope is 1.25m from the left post  Vertical position of the minimum height of the rope <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{31}{64}\\text{m}\\approx0.48\\text{m}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (2 SF)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>The rope ends at the top of the right post which is 2.5m from the left post. So <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x&gt;2.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> would be modelling beyond the length of the rope.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q4","number":4,"question":"<p>The path of a swimmer may be modelled by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"d=0.1x^2-x+0.9\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\geq0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"d\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the depth of the swimmer in relation to the water surface and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> metres is the distance from the poolside.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Sketch the graph of the model.</p></li></ol><p>According to the model:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"2\"><li><p>Find the depth that the swimmer swims below the surface of the water.</p></li><li><p>Determine the horizontal distance the swimmer swam under the water.</p></li><li><p>State how far from the pool edge the swimmer is when they first contact the water.</p></li><li><p>From what value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> does the function become an unrealistic model for the motion of the swimmer?</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.11/solutions/assets/diagrams/s4a.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac12(1+9)=5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=5,d=-1.6\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  1.6 metres below the surface.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>Between <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=9\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so 8 metres</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>1 m</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>After 9 metres from the poolside, the model suggests that the swimmer is leaving the water, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x&gt;9\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q5","number":5,"question":"<p>The income \u00a3<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span> made by a company on various cleaning services they provide may be modelled by the equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I=\\frac52x(40-x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where \u00a3<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the price charged for each individual service.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Sketch the model for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\\leq x\\leq40\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Rearrange the model into the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I=p-\\frac52(x-q)^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constants to be found.</p></li></ol><p>According to the model:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"3\"><li><p>Find the maximum income that is made.</p></li><li><p>Find the price of the service that gives the maximum income.</p></li><li><p>Find the prices which give an income of \u00a3600.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.11/solutions/assets/diagrams/s5a.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{40}2=20\\Rightarrow y=1000\" data-display=\"false\"></span> So <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I=1000-\\frac52(x-20)^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>\u00a31000</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>\u00a320</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1000-\\frac52(x-20)^2=600\\Rightarrow\\frac52(x-20)^2=400\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow(x-20)^2=160\\Rightarrow x=20\\pm\\sqrt{160}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\pounds7.35\" data-display=\"false\"></span> or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\pounds32.65\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q6","number":6,"question":"<p>The profit of a business selling books depends on the price at which each book is sold. The equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=\\frac{15}2x-\\frac{25}2-\\frac58x^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is used to model the relationship between the profit and the price where \u00a3<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the selling price of each book and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> pence is the profit achieved at that price. A sketch of the curve is shown in the diagram below:</p><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.11/questions/assets/diagrams/q6.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><p>Find</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>the selling price that generates the maximum profit.</p></li><li><p>the value of the maximum profit.</p></li><li><p>the selling price that gives no profit.</p></li><li><p>the loss if the books are given away.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=\\frac{15}2x-\\frac{25}2-\\frac58x^2=-\\frac58[x^2-12x+20]\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=-\\frac58[(x-6)^2-6^2+20]\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=-\\frac58[(x-6)^2-16]\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=-\\frac58(x-6)^2+10\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=6\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  Best profit when charging \u00a36 per book</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Max profit of \u00a30.10 per book</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\frac58[x^2-12x+20]=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2-12x+20=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x-2)(x-10)=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  No profit when charging \u00a32 or \u00a310 per book</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-intercept: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-12.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so a 12.5p loss per book</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q7","number":7,"question":"<p>The growth of two flowers may be modelled by quadratic curves as follow:  <u>Flower A:</u> height <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> cm after <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> days is given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac2{45}x^2+\\frac83x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.  <u>Flower B:</u> height <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> cm after <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> days is given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac1{10}x^2+3x+\\frac{15}2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Calculate the number of days which have passed when they are the same height.</p></li><li><p>Find the maximum height of the curve representing flower B.</p></li><li><p>Explain why the growth of the flowers may not best be modelled by these models.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\frac2{45}x^2+\\frac83x=-\\frac1{10}x^2+3x+\\frac{15}2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac1{18}x^2-\\frac13x-\\frac{15}2=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\frac{\\frac13\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac19+\\frac53}}{\\frac19}=\\frac{\\frac13\\pm\\frac43}{\\frac19}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=15\" data-display=\"false\"></span> or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=-9\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> can't be negative so at 15 days.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\frac1{10}x^2+3x+\\frac{15}2=-\\frac1{10}[x^2-30x-75]\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=-\\frac1{10}[(x-15)^2-(-15)^2-75]\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=-\\frac1{10}[(x-15)^2-300]=-\\frac1{10}(x-15)^2+30\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  So maximum height is 30 cm</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>The quadratics reach a maximum then reduce, whilst the plants will either keep growing or reach a fixed height.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q8","number":8,"question":"<p>A ball is projected from the ground. It reaches a maximum height of 10 metres at 2 seconds since being projected. Find a model for the trajectory of the ball in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=at^2+bt\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is height (metres), <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is time (seconds) and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constants to be found.</p>","steps":["<p>Max at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2,10)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=k(t-2)^2+10\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for some constant <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(0,0):0=k(-2)^2+10\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=-2.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=-2.5(t-2)^2+10\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=-2.5(t^2-4t+4)+10\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=-2.5t^2+10t-10+10\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=-2.5t^2+10t\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q9","number":9,"question":"<p>A stone is projected from the ground. It starts at a horizontal position of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> metres from a wall. It is modelled by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac18x^2+\\frac{11}8x-\\frac{57}{32}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are the horizontal and vertical distance in metres respectively from the bottom of the wall.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Find the total horizontal distance travelled by the ball before it hits the ground.</p></li><li><p>Find the maximum height that the ball reaches.</p></li><li><p>A tree of height 1.2 m is 7.5 m from the first wall, does the stone hit the tree?</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(a,0):0=-\\frac18a^2+\\frac{11}8a-\\frac{57}{32}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=1.5\\text{m}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=9.5\\text{m}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Since it is projected from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=1.5\\text{m}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"9.5-1.5=8\\text{m}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>Max occurs when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=5.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>,  so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac18(5.5)^2+\\frac{11}8(5.5)-\\frac{57}{32}=2\\text{m}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> above the ground</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=7.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> the ball is at height  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac18(7.5)^2+\\frac{11}8(7.5)-\\frac{57}{32}=1.5\\text{m}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> which is higher than the tree so it doesn't hit it.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.11-q10","number":10,"question":"<p>An arch has a span of 350m and a maximum vertical height of 140m above ground level. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> be the horizontal distance from the left-most point of the arch in metres and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> be the vertical distance above the ground. Find a model in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=\\frac pqx(r-x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are integers to be found.</p>","steps":["<p>Max point at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(175,140)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=k(x-175)^2+140\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for some constant <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  Left most arch start at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(0,0)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0=k(-175)^2+140\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=-\\frac4{875}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac4{875}(x-175)^2+140\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac4{875}(x^2-350x+30625)+140\" data-display=\"false\"></span>  <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-\\frac4{875}x^2+\\frac85x=\\frac4{875}x(350-x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>"]}]